::: {.doc-subject}

Check if an edge coloring is valid (no two edges sharing a node have the same color).
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L76067-L76174){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.is_valid_edge_coloring(coloring)
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Is valid edge coloring](../assets/graphs/is-valid-edge-coloring.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="The same triangle A, B, C with a tail C-D on both sides, each edge drawn in its color and labeled with the color number. Left: the colors 0, 1, 2 on the triangle and 0 on C-D never meet twice at a node, so is_valid_edge_coloring() returns True. Right: C-D is recolored to 1, the color of B-C, and the two meet at C (in the accent color), so is_valid_edge_coloring() returns False." width="100%"}

The edge counterpart of `is_valid_coloring()`: it checks that, at every node, the
colored edges meeting there all have different colors. Use it to check an edge coloring
built by hand, loaded from a file or edited after `edge_coloring()`.

A few details of the check:

- edge direction is ignored, and parallel edges count as sharing both of their nodes
- a self-loop meets its node only once, so it conflicts with the node's other edges
  but not with itself
- edges missing from `coloring=` are skipped, so a *partial* coloring is reported as
  valid when none of its colored edges conflict
- keys that are not edge IDs in the graph are ignored
:::

## Parameters {.doc-parameters}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code>[coloring]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [dict[int, int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   A dictionary mapping edge IDs to colors, such as the output of `edge_coloring()`.
    Only equality between colors matters.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   `True` if no two colored edges that share a node have the same color.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~edge_coloring`) 

:   Find an edge coloring with at most one more color than the maximum degree.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

In a triangle, every two edges share a node, so each needs its own color:

::: {#ef4a3f8f .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

triangle = ec.Graph().add_cycle_graph(3)
ab, bc, ca = sorted(triangle.get_edge_ids())

(
    triangle.is_valid_edge_coloring({ab: 0, bc: 1, ca: 2}),
    triangle.is_valid_edge_coloring({ab: 0, bc: 0, ca: 1}),
)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


### A league in five rounds

A league of six clubs plays a round robin, 15 games in all. `edge_coloring()` spreads
them over six rounds, but the circle method needs only five. It keeps one club in place
and turns the others around it, and each turn gives the next round:

::: {#16f81af1 .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
from itertools import combinations

clubs = ["Ash", "Birch", "Cedar", "Elm", "Fir", "Oak"]
league = ec.Graph.from_edges(list(combinations(clubs, 2)))

label = league.get_label_from_id
game = {
    frozenset([label(u), label(v)]): e
    for e, u, v in league.get_edges_as_tuples()
}

rounds = {}
for r in range(5):
    pairs = [(clubs[5], clubs[r])]
    pairs += [(clubs[(r + k) % 5], clubs[(r - k) % 5]) for k in (1, 2)]
    for pair in pairs:
        rounds[game[frozenset(pair)]] = r

len(rounds), league.is_valid_edge_coloring(rounds)
```

